# Lore, part 6: fuller entries that replace the short ones of parts 2 to 4 (second half).
LORE = {
'bodo': ("The Bodos are among the oldest peoples of the Brahmaputra valley, kin of the Kachari kings who ruled Assam before the Ahoms; in the late twentieth century they fought for, and won, a territorial council of their own.",
         "They dance the Bagurumba, the butterfly dance, weave the bright dokhona wrap, keep the Bathou faith around the sacred siju cactus, and grow rice and silk in the foothills."),
'wichi_qom': ("The Wichi and Qom are peoples of the Gran Chaco, hunters, fishers and gatherers of the dry forest who resisted the armies of Argentina and Paraguay into the twentieth century.",
              "Wichi women weave bags of chaguar fibre in patterns passed down for generations; the Qom are known for their Anglican and Pentecostal churches, their songs and their fight for land titles."),
'gurage_sidama': ("The Gurage, Sidama and Wolaita are peoples of the southern Ethiopian highlands; the kingdom of Wolaita was conquered by Menelik II in 1894.",
                  "They grow ensete, the false banana, whose fermented starch is their bread; the Sidama celebrate Fichee Chambalaalla, their new year, the Gurage are known as traders and for kitfo, and their coffee is among the best in the world."),
'sherpa': ("The Sherpas came from eastern Tibet to the Khumbu valley below Everest about five hundred years ago; from the 1920s they became the guides and climbers of the Himalaya, and Tenzing Norgay stood on the summit in 1953.",
           "They keep Tibetan Buddhism and the monastery of Tengboche, the masked Mani Rimdu festival, yak herding and potato farming at four thousand metres, and drink butter tea and chang."),
'senufo': ("The Senufo are farmers of northern Ivory Coast, Mali and Burkina Faso, organised in villages around the Poro society, which educates young men over many years.",
           "Their carvers make some of Africa's most admired masks and figures, their balafon players are famous, and the Fakaha painters of Korhogo cloth draw spirits and animals on cotton."),
'yazidi': ("The Yazidis are a Kurdish-speaking people of northern Iraq with an ancient faith of their own; they suffered many persecutions and genocide by the Islamic State in 2014.",
           "They pilgrimage to the holy valley of Lalish, revere Melek Taus, the peacock angel, celebrate their new year in April with coloured eggs, and marry only within their community."),
'tigrinya': ("The Tigrinya live in the highlands of Eritrea and Tigray, heirs of Aksum, one of the great empires of antiquity, which minted coins and became Christian in the fourth century.",
             "They keep the Orthodox faith with its ancient Ge'ez liturgy, rock-hewn churches, the coffee ceremony with three rounds, injera with zigni, and the shoulder-shaking dance."),
'circassian': ("The Circassians held the north-western Caucasus until Russia's long war of conquest ended in 1864 with the expulsion of most of them to the Ottoman empire; the diaspora is larger than the homeland.",
               "They keep Adyghe Xabze, a strict code of honour, respect for elders and hospitality, dance the elegant Circassian dances, and remember 21 May, the day of mourning."),
'mon': ("The Mon are among the oldest peoples of mainland Southeast Asia; their kingdoms of Dvaravati, Thaton and Hanthawaddy brought Theravada Buddhism to the region, before the Burmese conquest of 1757.",
        "Their script and monks shaped Burmese and Thai culture, they keep Buddhist festivals and the Mon new year, and eat a famous rice dish, thingyan rice, with fish."),
'pamiri': ("The Pamiris live in the high valleys of the Pamirs at the roof of the world; they are Ismaili Muslims who follow the Aga Khan, and speak Eastern Iranian languages related to the ancient Saka.",
           "Their chid houses have five pillars standing for holy figures, they play the rubab and sing the madah religious songs, and they are known for their education and their hospitality."),
'ladakhi': ("The Ladakhis and Baltis live in the high deserts of the Himalaya; Ladakh was a Buddhist kingdom tied to Tibet, and Baltistan became Shia Muslim and was ruled by its own rajas.",
            "Ladakhis hold masked cham dances at the monasteries of Hemis and Lamayuru, grow barley and apricots, and drink butter tea; the Baltis play polo in the original wild style."),
'beja': ("The Beja are Cushitic herders of the Red Sea hills between Egypt and Eritrea, among the oldest peoples of the region; Kipling's poem praised the Hadendoa who broke a British square in 1884.",
         "They herd camels, wear swords and the distinctive hair style, drink jabana coffee seasoned with ginger, and are famous for their dignity and generosity."),
'makua': ("The Makua are the largest people of northern Mozambique; their coast was part of the Swahili world, the island of Mozambique was a Portuguese capital, and the slave trade took many.",
          "Makua women wear the white mussiro face mask for beauty, they dance the tufo, and their matrilineal clans guide inheritance and marriage."),
'mapuche': ("The Mapuche resisted the Incas and stopped the Spanish at the Bio Bio river for three centuries; Chile and Argentina conquered them only in the 1880s.",
            "They play palin, a field hockey-like game, are healed by the machi, women shamans, hold the nguillatun prayer ceremony, and their silver jewellery and textiles are famous."),
'ni_vanuatu': ("The Ni-Vanuatu and Solomon Islanders are Melanesian peoples; Vanuatu was ruled jointly by Britain and France until 1980, and the Solomons saw the battle of Guadalcanal.",
               "On Pentecost men land-dive from wooden towers with vines on their ankles, they hold kava ceremonies, and the Solomons still use shell money in bride price."),
'ndyuka': ("The Ndyuka and Saramaka are Maroons who escaped from the plantations of Suriname and signed treaties with the Dutch in the 1760s, keeping their freedom in the forest.",
           "They carve beautiful wooden paddles and combs, speak English-based creoles, keep African-derived religions and paint their houses."),
'bamileke': ("The Bamileke and Grassfielders are the peoples of the western highlands of Cameroon, organised in chiefdoms under the fon; they fought for independence in the 1950s and 1960s.",
             "Their chiefs' palaces are museums of art, with beaded thrones and elephant masks; they are known as the best entrepreneurs of Cameroon, and their tontines, savings circles, finance everything."),
'mbundu': ("The Mbundu kingdom of Ndongo resisted the Portuguese for decades under Queen Nzinga in the seventeenth century, one of the greatest leaders of African history.",
           "Kimbundu is their language, Luanda lies in their land, and they created semba music, mother of kizomba, and the carnival of Luanda."),
'baoule': ("The Baoule are an Akan people who migrated west to Ivory Coast in the eighteenth century under Queen Abla Pokou, who by legend sacrificed her child to cross the Comoe river.",
           "They carve fine masks and portrait figures, weave cloth, grow cocoa and coffee, and the first president of Ivory Coast, Houphouet-Boigny, was a Baoule."),
'maithili': ("The Maithils of Mithila, the land of Sita and King Janaka, have one of India's oldest literatures, with the fourteenth-century poet Vidyapati, and their language is official in India and Nepal.",
             "Their women paint Madhubani art on walls and paper, they keep Chhath and Sama-Chakeva, and their cooking of fish, makhana lotus seeds and mangoes is famous."),
'munduruku': ("The Munduruku of the Tapajos river were feared warriors who once ruled a large part of the Amazon and kept the heads of enemies as trophies.",
              "Today they defend their river against dams and illegal gold mines, keep their festivals, and their traditional knowledge of the forest."),
'banyarwanda': ("The Banyarwanda and Barundi are the peoples of the kingdoms of Rwanda and Burundi, with mwami kings and royal drums; the genocide of 1994 killed most of the Tutsi of Rwanda.",
                "They dance intore with long-haired headdresses, play the royal drums of Burundi, and keep cattle in high esteem, giving cows as the highest gift."),
'chaoui_rif': ("The Chaouis of the Aures mountains and the Riffians of the Rif are Berber peoples; Abd el-Krim's Rif Republic defeated Spain in the 1920s, and the Aures began the Algerian war of independence in 1954.",
               "They keep the Berber language, mountain festivals, rich silver jewellery, and the Rif is known for its fierce independence."),
'sardinian': ("Sardinia's thousands of nuraghe towers date from the Bronze Age; the island was ruled by Pisa, Aragon and Savoy, and its language is the closest to Latin of all Romance tongues.",
              "They sing cantu a tenore, a polyphony of four men, roast suckling pig, porceddu, keep the masks of the Mamoiada carnival, and are among the longest-living people on Earth."),
'macedonian': ("The Macedonians are South Slavs of the Vardar valley; their land was part of the Ottoman empire for five centuries, rose in the Ilinden uprising of 1903, and became a republic of Yugoslavia and then independent in 1991.",
               "They make tavce gravce beans and ajvar, sing folk songs in odd rhythms, dance the oro, and Lake Ohrid with its churches is their jewel."),
'garifuna': ("The Garifuna descend from Africans and Island Caribs of St Vincent, deported by the British to Central America in 1797; the Miskito of the coast were allies of Britain.",
             "They dance punta, keep their language and drums, and the Dugu ancestor ceremony; their story is a UNESCO masterpiece."),
'maasai': ("The Maasai are Nilotic herders of the Rift Valley who once dominated the plains of Kenya and Tanzania, and lost much of their land to colonial settlers and parks.",
           "They wear red shuka cloth and beadwork, jump in the adumu dance, pass through age-sets as warriors, moran, and drink milk and blood from their cattle."),
'lur': ("The Lurs and Bakhtiari are Iranian peoples of the Zagros mountains, famous for their seasonal migrations with flocks across high passes and rivers.",
        "They dance with handkerchiefs to the sorna and dohol, weave gabbeh rugs, and keep the Lur epics and laments."),
'odia': ("The Odias of Kalinga fought the Mauryan emperor Ashoka in a war so bloody that it turned him to Buddhism; later the Ganga kings built the temples of Puri and Konark.",
         "They hold the Rath Yatra, the chariot festival of Jagannath, dance the classical Odissi, and eat pakhala, fermented rice, and the rasagola they claim as theirs."),
'ovambo': ("The Ovambo are the largest people of Namibia, farmers of the north; they led the SWAPO fight against South African rule until independence in 1990.",
           "They pound mahangu millet, their women wear pink dresses on festive days, and their kingdoms still have kings."),
'fijian': ("The Fijians are Melanesian and Polynesian; Fiji was united under Cakobau and ceded to Britain in 1874, which brought Indian indentured labourers.",
           "They drink yaqona, kava, in ceremonies, play rugby with passion, and live in villages with chiefs, the bure and the lovo earth oven."),
'tlingit_yupik': ("The Tlingit, Yup'ik and other Alaska Natives have lived in Alaska for millennia; the Tlingit fought the Russians at Sitka in 1802 and 1804.",
                  "They carve totem poles, hold potlatches, weave Chilkat blankets, and the Yup'ik dance with masks and hunt seals."),
'buryat': ("The Buryats are a Mongol people around Lake Baikal, absorbed by Russia in the seventeenth century, who became Buddhists while keeping shamanism.",
           "They celebrate Sagaalgan, eat buuza dumplings, revere Baikal as sacred, and their datsan monasteries were rebuilt after Soviet times."),
'tujia': ("The Tujia of the Wuling mountains were ruled by hereditary native chieftains until the Qing replaced them in the eighteenth century.",
          "They weave xilankapu brocade, dance the hand-waving dance, and brides sing crying songs before their weddings."),
'ewe': ("The Ewe migrated from Notsie to the coast of Togo and Ghana in the seventeenth century, and their land was divided by colonial borders between Britain and Germany.",
        "They weave their own kente, drum and dance agbadza, celebrate Hogbetsotso in memory of their escape, and keep Vodun."),
'visayan': ("The Visayans of the central Philippines met Magellan at Cebu in 1521, where Lapulapu killed him at Mactan, the first victory against Europe in Asia.",
            "They celebrate the Sinulog and Ati-Atihan festivals for the Santo Nino, roast lechon, and speak Cebuano and Hiligaynon."),
'cajun': ("The Acadians of Nova Scotia were expelled by the British in 1755; many settled in the bayous of Louisiana and became the Cajuns.",
          "They play Cajun and zydeco music with accordion and fiddle, cook gumbo, jambalaya and crawfish boils, and hold Mardi Gras runs in the countryside."),
'avar': ("The Avars and Dagestani peoples live in the mountains of Dagestan, where Imam Shamil led the resistance against Russia for twenty-five years.",
         "They are famous wrestlers, make carpets, silver and pottery, and live in stone villages perched on cliffs."),
'mizo_chin': ("The Mizo and Chin live in the hills between India and Burma; British missionaries made them largely Christian, and the Mizo National Front fought for independence in the 1960s.",
              "They dance cheraw between clapping bamboo poles, weave puan cloth, and sing hymns in great choirs."),
'moldovan': ("The Moldovans are Romanian-speaking people of Bessarabia, annexed by Russia in 1812 and by the Soviet Union in 1940; Moldova became independent in 1991.",
             "They are wine-makers with vast underground cellars like Milestii Mici, and eat mamaliga and placinte."),
'lunda_chokwe': ("The Lunda empire of the Mwata Yamvo ruled Central Africa in the eighteenth century; the Chokwe hunters and traders rose after it in the nineteenth.",
                 "The Chokwe carve masks of Mwana Pwo, the ideal young woman, and the Lunda kings still hold their ceremonies."),
'songhai': ("The Songhai empire ruled the Niger bend from Gao in the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries under Sonni Ali and Askia Muhammad, before falling to Morocco in 1591.",
            "They fish and farm the Niger, and the manuscripts of Timbuktu, saved from destruction in 2012, are their pride."),
'yanomami': ("The Yanomami of the Guiana highlands lived in isolation until the twentieth century, when gold miners invaded their land and brought disease and violence.",
             "They live in round communal houses, the shabono, hunt and garden, and their shamans speak with the xapiri spirits."),
'guyanese': ("The Guyanese and Surinamese are peoples of Indian, African, Javanese, Chinese, Amerindian and European roots, made by British and Dutch plantations.",
             "They eat pepperpot and roti, play cricket, and celebrate Diwali, Phagwah, Eid, Christmas and Mashramani."),
'wolof': ("The Wolof kingdoms of Jolof, Kayor and Baol ruled Senegal; the Mouride Sufi brotherhood was founded among them by Amadou Bamba in the late nineteenth century.",
          "They dance to mbalax, eat thieboudienne, rice with fish, and make the great pilgrimage to Touba."),
'tatar': ("The Volga Tatars are heirs of Volga Bulgaria and the Golden Horde; the Kazan Khanate fell to Ivan the Terrible in 1552.",
          "They celebrate Sabantuy with wrestling and races, eat chak-chak and echpochmak, and keep Islam on the Volga."),
'chukchi': ("The Chukchi and Koryaks fought the Russians for a long time; they are reindeer herders and sea hunters of the far north-east.",
            "They hunt whales and walrus, herd reindeer, and carve walrus ivory."),
'greenlander': ("The New Greenlanders are a mixed people of Inuit, Danes and newcomers who settled the thawing island in the twenty-second century.",
                "They fish, mine, farm in sheltered fjords, and keep the Inuit drum dance alive alongside new festivals."),
'alaskan': ("Alaska was bought by the United States from Russia in 1867; the gold rush, the war and the oil of the twentieth century made it.",
            "They fish salmon, race sled dogs in the Iditarod, and live under the northern lights."),
'latvian': ("The Latvians are a Baltic people ruled by German knights for centuries; Latvia became independent in 1918 and again in 1991.",
            "They hold the great song and dance festival, Jani at midsummer with wreaths and cheese, and eat rye bread."),
'rakhine': ("The Rakhine had their own kingdom of Mrauk U, which traded with the Portuguese and Dutch, until the Burmese conquest of 1784.",
            "They celebrate the Thingyan water festival, and are known for their Buddhist art and the Mahamuni image."),
'purepecha': ("The Purepecha empire of Michoacan resisted the Aztecs and was never conquered by them.",
              "They make copper, lacquer and pottery, dance the Dance of the Old Men, and hold the Night of the Dead on Lake Patzcuaro."),
'druze': ("The Druze faith arose in eleventh-century Egypt; the Druze live in the mountains of Lebanon, Syria and Israel, and played a great role in Lebanese history.",
          "Their faith is secret, they keep strong solidarity and hospitality, and they are known for their cooking and their white headgear."),
'tsonga': ("The Tsonga live in southern Mozambique and South Africa; the Gaza empire of the Nguni ruled them in the nineteenth century.",
           "They dance xibelani with swirling skirts, and their music is famous across South Africa."),
'slovak': ("The Slovaks lived for a thousand years under the Kingdom of Hungary; they joined the Czechs in 1918 and became independent in 1993.",
           "They make bryndzove halusky, and are proud of folk music, the fujara flute and wooden churches."),
'ashaninka': ("The Ashaninka of the Peruvian Amazon resisted Spanish missions and survived the violence of the Shining Path in the 1980s and 1990s.",
              "They wear the cushma tunic, paint their faces with achiote, and drink masato."),
'walloon': ("The Walloons are the French-speaking people of southern Belgium, heirs of the first industrial revolution on the continent.",
            "They keep the giants processions, the Gilles of Binche, eat boulets and waffles, and brew Trappist beer."),
'copt': ("The Copts are the Christians of Egypt, heirs of the ancient Egyptians; their church was founded, by tradition, by Saint Mark.",
         "They keep long fasts, the desert monasteries, and the Coptic liturgy keeps the last form of the Egyptian language."),
'meitei': ("The Meitei of Manipur had their own kingdom for two thousand years, with its chronicle the Cheitharol Kumbaba.",
           "They dance the Manipuri Ras Lila, play polo, which they gave the world, and hold the Lai Haraoba festival."),
'polynesian': ("The Pacific Islanders are heirs of the Lapita voyagers and Polynesian navigators who found every island of the Pacific without instruments.",
               "They tattoo, dance with drums, build canoes, and cook in earth ovens."),
'kashmiri': ("Kashmir was a centre of Hindu and Buddhist learning before Islam came; it has been disputed since 1947.",
             "They weave pashmina shawls and carpets, hold the wazwan feast, drink kahwa tea, and live on houseboats on Dal Lake."),
}
